WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.020
A safe parser begins with a clear command-line contract.

2
00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:08.920
Before writing parsing branches, define the interface the tool intends to support.

3
00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:10.940
Which options are required?

4
00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:12.320
Which are optional?

5
00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:14.660
Which flags are booleans?

6
00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:16.520
Which options take values?

7
00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:20.700
Which arguments are positional, and what order do they use?

8
00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:23.140
Defaults should be explicit.

9
00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:31.800
If an output directory, verbosity level, timeout, mode, or limit is optional, the program should document the default and apply it consistently.

10
00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:37.020
Hidden defaults make parser behavior hard to review and hard for scripts to depend on.

11
00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:39.860
Duplicate handling needs a contract.

12
00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:43.320
Some tools reject duplicate options.

13
00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:45.580
Some allow the last value to win.

14
00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:48.640
Some accumulate repeated values into a list.

15
00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:54.100
Any of those choices can be reasonable, but accidental behavior is not.

16
00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:59.400
Reviewers should be able to see what happens when the same option appears twice.

17
00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:05.520
Mutual exclusions and dependencies should be documented before parsing logic becomes tangled.

18
00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:09.780
If two modes cannot be used together, reject that combination clearly.

19
00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:15.480
If one option requires another option, check the relationship after parsing and before action.

20
00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:17.820
Cross-option rules are part of validation.

21
00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:20.820
Usage messages are also part of the contract.

22
00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:24.760
A predictable message tells users and automation what the tool expects.

23
00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:33.740
It should describe options, values, positionals, and failure behavior without exposing sensitive runtime data or internal implementation details.

24
00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:38.260
The more explicit the option contract is, the easier it is to test.

25
00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:47.240
Unit tests can cover required arguments, missing values, duplicates, defaults, exclusivity, dependencies, and unknown options.

26
00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:51.740
That turns parser design into something repeatable rather than tribal memory.
